.TH CURLDBG 1 "August 2026" "curldbg 2.1.7" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
curldbg \- lightweight HTTP/HTTPS client and request debugger
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B curldbg
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIurl\fR
.P
.B curldbg
\-\-compare [\fIoptions\fR] \fIurl\fR
.P
.B curldbg
\-\-compare-urls [\fIoptions\fR] \fIurl-a\fR \fIurl-b\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B curldbg
performs a single HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2 request (GET/POST/PUT/HEAD) with automatic
ALPN-based HTTP/2 negotiation, HPACK header compression (Huffman encode+decode),
and full multiplexing support. It reports per-hop timing metrics (DNS, TCP connect,
TTFB, total) and prints a short preview of the response body. It can follow
redirects, track the full redirect chain with per-hop timing, compare IPv4 vs IPv6,
or compare two URLs side-by-side.
.P
When stdout is not a TTY, curldbg auto-silences normal output and writes the raw
response body to stdout (pipe-friendly). If no URL scheme is given, https:// is
assumed.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Request control"
.TP
.B \-X, \-\-request \fIMETHOD\fR
Choose HTTP method (default: GET). Supports GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, HEAD, PATCH, OPTIONS, PURGE, and others.
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-data \fIbody\fR
Send request body. Defaults method to POST if \-X is not set.
Prefix with @ to read from a file (\fB@file\fR) or stdin (\fB@\-\fR).
Repeatable \(em values are concatenated with &.
.TP
.B \-\-data-binary
Alias for \-d.
.TP
.B \-\-data-urlencode \fIdata\fR
URL-encode and send request data. Accepts \fBname=content\fR (name is left
as-is, content is encoded), \fB@file\fR, or \fB=content\fR (raw content is
encoded). Defaults method to POST if \-X is not set.
.TP
.B \-T, \-\-upload-file \fIfile\fR
Upload file as the PUT request body (single request mode only).
.TP
.B \-I, \-\-head
Perform a HEAD request (headers only, no body).
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-fail
Exit with non-zero status on HTTP status >= 400.
.SS "Output control"
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-silent
Suppress normal timing/output display.
.TP
.B \-S, \-\-show-error
Show error messages even when \-s is active.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose
Print request headers (> prefix) and response headers (< prefix) to stderr.
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-output \fIfile\fR
Write response body to file (single request mode only).
.TP
.B \-O, \-\-remote-name
Write response body using the URL's filename component (single request mode only).
.TP
.B \-D, \-\-dump-header \fIfile\fR
Write the response headers to \fIfile\fR.
.TP
.B \-w, \-\-write-out \fIformat\fR
Print the given format string after the request completes. Supports
\fB%{http_code}\fR, \fB%{time_total}\fR, \fB%{time_namelookup}\fR,
\fB%{time_connect}\fR, \fB%{time_starttransfer}\fR, \fB%{url_effective}\fR,
\fB%{num_redirects}\fR, \fB%{http_version}\fR, and \fB%{redirect_url}\fR, as
well as \fB\\n\fR, \fB\\r\fR, \fB\\t\fR escapes.
.TP
.B \-\-progress-bar
Accepted for curl compatibility (no-op).
.SS "TLS / Auth"
.TP
.B \-k, \-\-insecure
Skip TLS certificate verification.
.TP
.B \-u, \-\-user \fIuser:pass\fR
Use HTTP Basic authentication.
.TP
.B \-A, \-\-user-agent \fIstring\fR
Set a custom User-Agent header (default: curldbg/<version>).
.TP
.B \-\-cacert \fIfile\fR
Use \fIfile\fR as the CA certificate bundle for TLS verification.
.TP
.B \-\-capath \fIdir\fR
Use \fIdir\fR as a directory of CA certificates (hashed) for TLS verification.
.TP
.B \-\-tlsv1.2
Force TLS 1.2 (disable TLS 1.3).
.TP
.B \-\-tlsv1.3
Force TLS 1.3 (disable TLS 1.2).
.SS "Headers"
.TP
.B \-H, \-\-header \fIheader\fR
Add a request header (repeatable). Providing Content-Type, Content-Length, or
Host via \-H skips auto-generation of those headers.
.TP
.B \-e, \-\-referer \fIurl\fR
Set the Referer request header.
.SS "Connectivity"
.TP
.B \-4
Force IPv4 DNS resolution and connect.
.TP
.B \-6
Force IPv6 DNS resolution and connect.
.TP
.B \-\-no-happy-eyeballs
Disable Happy Eyeballs; connect sequentially (IPv6 then IPv4).
.TP
.B \-\-connect-timeout \fIms\fR
Timeout per connect attempt in milliseconds.
.TP
.B \-\-read-timeout \fIms\fR
Timeout for read/write operations in milliseconds.
.TP
.B \-\-max-time \fIms\fR
Total operation timeout in milliseconds (applied across all phases including
redirects).
.TP
.B \-\-retry \fIn\fR
Retry the request up to \fIn\fR times on failure (default: 0).
.TP
.B \-\-retry-delay \fIs\fR
Delay in seconds between retries (default: 0).
.TP
.B \-\-resolve \fIhost:port:addr\fR
Provide a custom address for a specific host and port, bypassing DNS
(repeatable). \fIaddr\fR may be an IPv4 or IPv6 address (bracket IPv6 as
\fB[::1]\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-interface \fIaddr\fR
Bind the connection to a specific local IP address or network interface.
.TP
.B \-\-unix-socket \fIpath\fR
Connect through a Unix domain socket instead of TCP (e.g. to reach a local
daemon such as the Docker socket). The URL host/port are still used for the
Host header.
.TP
.B \-\-http1.1
Force HTTP/1.1 (error if the server negotiates HTTP/2).
.TP
.B \-\-http2
Force HTTP/2 (error if the server does not negotiate it).
.SS "Compression"
.TP
.B \-\-compressed
Request a compressed response (adds Accept-Encoding: gzip, deflate) and
decompress the body automatically.
.SS "Redirects"
.TP
.B \-L, \-\-location
Follow redirects.
.TP
.B \-\-max-redirs \fIn\fR
Maximum number of redirects when \-L is active (default: 10).
.SS "Proxy"
.TP
.B \-\-proxy \fIurl\fR
Use an HTTP proxy (e.g. http://proxy:8080). Supports CONNECT tunneling for HTTPS targets.
.SS "Cookies"
.TP
.B \-b, \-\-cookie \fIstring\fR
Send cookies. Repeatable (values accumulate). Prefix with @ to load from a Netscape-format cookie file.
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-cookie-jar \fIfile\fR
Write Set-Cookie response headers to a file in Netscape cookie format.
.SS "Compare modes"
.TP
.B \-\-compare
Run the same URL over IPv4 and IPv6 concurrently and print side-by-side metrics
with deltas.
.TP
.B \-\-compare-urls
Run two different URLs concurrently and print side-by-side metrics with deltas.
.SS "Miscellaneous"
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show usage and available options.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Show version and author.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-disable
Accepted for curl compatibility (no-op; no .curlrc support).
.TP
.B \-\-proto \fIprotocols\fR
Accepted for curl compatibility (no-op).
.TP
.B \-\-proto-redir \fIprotocols\fR
Accepted for curl compatibility (no-op).
.SH "OUTPUT FIELDS"
When not in silent mode, curldbg prints:
.TP
.B dns
DNS resolution time in ms.
.TP
.B tcp
TCP connect time in ms.
.TP
.B ttfb
Time to first byte of the response in ms.
.TP
.B total
Total request time in ms.
.TP
.B connected
Connected IP address and address family (IPv4/IPv6).
.TP
.B final url
The final URL after any redirects.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B curldbg https://example.com
Basic GET request with timing output.
.TP
.B curldbg \-L https://httpbin.org/redirect/3
Follow redirects and show the full chain with per-hop timing.
.TP
.B curldbg \-u user:pass \-H "Accept: application/json" https://example.com
Basic auth with a custom Accept header.
.TP
.B curldbg \-X POST \-H "Content-Type: application/json" \-d '{"key":"value"}' https://api.example.com
JSON POST request.
.TP
.B curldbg \-d @data.json https://api.example.com
POST JSON from a file.
.TP
.B curldbg -sS -I https://example.com
Silent HEAD request (check headers only).
.TP
.B curldbg \-\-compare https://example.com
Compare IPv4 vs IPv6 performance.
.SH TESTING
Run the unit-test suite with:
.P
.B make test
.P
This builds the internal unit tests and runs them under Valgrind.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.IP 0
Success.
.IP 1
Generic error (DNS failure, connection refused, TLS error, timeout, etc.).
.SH AUTHOR
Pau Santana.
